WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.625
Keyword match types determine how closely the keywords you bid on match the shopping queries customers

00:00:01.625 --> 00:00:01.828
are using.

00:00:01.828 --> 00:00:02.946
So let's see when you might want to use Phrase Match.

00:00:02.946 --> 00:00:04.571
Take a set of blue cotton sheets that you're advertising and you've included blue cotton sheets

00:00:04.571 --> 00:00:04.876
as a keyword.

00:00:04.876 --> 00:00:06.602
If you're using the Phrase Match Type, your ad may display when customers are browsing for items

00:00:06.602 --> 00:00:07.618
using all three of those terms – blue, cotton, and sheets.

00:00:07.618 --> 00:00:30.336
In this order, plus close variations, like plurals.

00:00:30.336 --> 00:00:35.720
If customers' shopping queries include other terms before or after, your ad may also be eligible

00:00:35.720 --> 00:00:36.356
to appear.

00:00:36.356 --> 00:00:41.440
But if they include these terms in a different order, or not as a complete phrase,

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:42.980
your ad wouldn't be shown.

00:00:42.980 --> 00:00:46.060
Use Phrase Match after you've reviewed your search term reports

00:00:46.060 --> 00:00:48.749
to see how shoppers are browsing for your items.

00:00:48.749 --> 00:00:50.840
It's best for more specific keywords, also

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:54.840
called long-tail keywords, to help drive consideration and conversion.
